#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064023 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "0c234eb8-4a5b-52e1-8854-c183b68184bd" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Position of a Particle Given Its Velocity and Initial Position"}] :question/id #uuid "442b883a-d22b-5dda-8f8a-9eead6ae94b1" :question/math-academy-id "e-566" :question/problem "A particle moves along a straight line relative to a fixed origin $O$ with velocity $v(t) = 2t+3t^2,$ where $t\\geq 0$ is the time. If the particle is at the position $x=1$ when $t=0,$ calculate the position $x$ of the particle at time $t.$" :question/worked-solution "We start by integrating the velocity to get $x(t)\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(t) & =\\int v(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\int (2t+3{t}^{2})\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={t}^{2}+{t}^{3}+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo determine $C,$ we use the fact that $x(0) = 1.$ Substituting this into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}1 & =(0)^{2}+(0)^{3}+C \\\\ C & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the position $x$ of the particle at time $t$ is\n\n$$\nx(t) = t^3+t^2+1.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064024 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "43e68991-37f0-543e-a4da-b78b395a8494" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Position of a Particle Given Its Acceleration, Initial Velocity and Initial Position"}] :question/id #uuid "aaa51bb9-cde1-5062-ac75-0f35d6af1f53" :question/math-academy-id "e-567" :question/problem "A particle moves along a straight line relative to a fixed origin $O$ with acceleration $a(t) = 1-2t,$ where $t\\geq 0$ is the time. If the particle has position $x=1$ and velocity $v=2$ when $t=1,$ calculate the position $x$ of the particle at time $t >0.$" :question/worked-solution "We start by integrating the acceleration to get the velocity $v(t)\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =\\int a(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\int (1-2t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =t-{t}^{2}+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo determine $C,$ we use the fact that $v(1) = 2.$ Substituting this into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2 & =(1)-(1)^{2}+C \\\\ C & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo the velocity is given by $v(t) = t-t^2 + 2.$\n\nNow, we integrate the velocity to get $x(t)\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(t) & =\\int v(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\int (t-{t}^{2}+2)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{t}^{2}-\\frac{1}{3}{t}^{3}+2t+K\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo determine $K,$ we use the fact that $x(1) = 1.$ Substituting this into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}1 & =\\frac{1}{2}(1)^{2}-\\frac{1}{3}(1)^{3}+2(1)+K \\\\ K & =-\\frac{7}{6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the position $x$ of the particle at time $t$ is \n\n$$\nx(t) = \\dfrac{1}{2}t^2-\\dfrac{1}{3}t^3 +2t -\\dfrac{7}{6}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186289954 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "0c234eb8-4a5b-52e1-8854-c183b68184bd" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Position of a Particle Given Its Velocity and Initial Position"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "eb7675da-3715-50c7-8e7e-0edb9d8056e9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1-\\cot(t)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289956}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "eb7675da-3715-50c7-8e7e-0edb9d8056e9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1-\\cot(t)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289956} :answer-field/id #uuid "f6a8c2fc-1baf-5461-8089-f41525dae5c2" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289955}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "d598999c-b003-52da-8100-10acae05a1ed" :question/math-academy-id "q-351724" :question/problem "A particle moves along a straight line relative to a fixed origin $O$ with velocity $v(t)={csc}^{2}⁡(t),$ where $t>0$ is the time. If the particle is at the position $x=0$ when $t=\\frac{\\pi }{4},$ calculate the position $x$ of the particle at time $t.$\n\n$x(t)=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "We start by integrating the velocity to get $x(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(t) & =\\int v(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\int {csc}^{2}⁡(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =-\\operatorname{cot}⁡(t)+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo determine $C$, we use the fact that $x(\\frac{\\pi }{4})=0.$ Substituting this into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}0 & =-\\operatorname{cot}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4})+C \\\\ 0 & =-1+C \\\\ C & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the position $x$ of the particle at time $t$ is \n\n$$\nx(t)=-\\operatorname{cot}⁡(t)+1.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186289957 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "43e68991-37f0-543e-a4da-b78b395a8494" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Position of a Particle Given Its Acceleration, Initial Velocity and Initial Position"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f16f387d-edce-54ee-8767-0b82d1c97ee7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{e^{3t}}{9}+\\frac{2t}{3}-\\frac{1}{9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289959} {:answer/id #uuid "19414c18-04a3-56f6-849d-f4dca4fd12ba" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{e^{3t}}{9}+\\frac{2t}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289960}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f16f387d-edce-54ee-8767-0b82d1c97ee7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{e^{3t}}{9}+\\frac{2t}{3}-\\frac{1}{9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289959} :answer-field/id #uuid "d25ac9b8-b806-565e-8499-3284b9f5ebcc" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289958}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "273d6d87-8669-5f51-96f8-3d528dae9efc" :question/math-academy-id "q-351730" :question/problem "A particle moves along a straight line relative to a fixed origin $O$ with acceleration $a(t)={e}^{3t},$ where $t\\ge 0$ is the time. If the particle has position $x=0$ and velocity $v=1$ when $t=0,$ the position $x$ of the particle at time $t$ is\n\n$x(t)=$\n{{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "We start by integrating the acceleration to get the velocity $v(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =\\int a(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\int {e}^{3t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}{e}^{3t}+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo determine $C,$ we use the fact that $v(0)=1.$ Substituting this into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}1 & =\\frac{1}{3}{e}^{0}+C \\\\ 1 & =\\frac{1}{3}+C \\\\ C & =\\frac{2}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo the velocity is given by $v(t)=\\frac{1}{3}{e}^{3t}+\\frac{2}{3}.$\n\nNow, we integrate the velocity to get $x(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(t) & =\\int v(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\int (\\frac{1}{3}{e}^{3t}+\\frac{2}{3})\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\int {e}^{3t}\\,\\text{d}t+\\frac{2}{3}\\int \\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{9}{e}^{3t}+\\frac{2}{3}t+K\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo determine $K$, we use the fact that $x(0)=0.$ Substituting this into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}0 & =\\frac{1}{9}{e}^{0}+\\frac{2}{3}(0)+K \\\\ 0 & =\\frac{1}{9}+K \\\\ K & =-\\frac{1}{9}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the position $x$ of the particle at time $t$ is \n\n$$\nx(t)=\\frac{1}{9}{e}^{3t}+\\frac{2}{3}t-\\frac{1}{9}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186289961 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "43e68991-37f0-543e-a4da-b78b395a8494" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Position of a Particle Given Its Acceleration, Initial Velocity and Initial Position"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5c85da6b-693e-5b55-8b2b-cb592647c4b1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6t^2+t-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289963}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5c85da6b-693e-5b55-8b2b-cb592647c4b1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6t^2+t-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289963} :answer-field/id #uuid "a588a670-231e-5605-95a4-a382ffcf11db" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289962}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "af944fef-addc-5ba8-99ae-2326f2684b0e" :question/math-academy-id "q-351742" :question/problem "A particle moves along a straight line relative to a fixed origin $O$ with acceleration $a(t)=20{t}^{3}+12,$ where $t\\ge 0$ is the time. If the particle has position $x=-3$ and velocity $v=1$ when $t=0,$ the position $x$ of the particle at time $t$ is\n\n$x(t)={t}^{5}+\\,$\n{{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "We start by integrating the acceleration to get the velocity $v(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =\\int a(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\int (20{t}^{3}+12)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =5{t}^{4}+12t+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo determine $C,$ we use the fact that $v(0)=1.$ Substituting this into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}1 & =0+0+C \\\\ C & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo the velocity is given by $v(t)=5{t}^{4}+12t+1.$\n\nNow, we integrate the velocity to get $x(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(t) & =\\int v(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\int (5{t}^{4}+12t+1)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={t}^{5}+6{t}^{2}+t+K\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo determine $K$, we use the fact that $x(0)=-3.$ Substituting this into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-3 & =0+0+0+K \\\\ K & =-3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the position $x$ of the particle at time $t$ is \n\n$$\nx(t)={t}^{5}+6{t}^{2}+t-3.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186289964 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "0c234eb8-4a5b-52e1-8854-c183b68184bd" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Position of a Particle Given Its Velocity and Initial Position"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "fda4b7f1-5fc0-5fc4-aa86-a0c8d5bd0d79" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x(t)=4{t}^{3}+\\frac{{t}^{3}}{3}+6t+\\frac{20}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289966} {:answer/id #uuid "4c6bb769-15ed-5ef1-9fd1-b892437d6769" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x(t)={t}^{4}+{t}^{2}+6t-\\frac{20}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289967} {:answer/id #uuid "2e129c55-9fa8-5b4e-846b-7cc3246ac7af" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x(t)={t}^{4}+\\frac{{t}^{3}}{3}+3{t}^{2}+\\frac{20}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289968} {:answer/id #uuid "2e15f3fd-5fe0-57f4-91cc-df909c37fa20" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x(t)=4{t}^{3}+\\frac{{t}^{3}}{3}+3{t}^{2}+\\frac{20}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289969} {:answer/id #uuid "7681f6e8-66b9-5711-ae05-3ec0c6c21856" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x(t)={t}^{4}+\\frac{{t}^{3}}{3}+3{t}^{2}-\\frac{20}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289970}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2e129c55-9fa8-5b4e-846b-7cc3246ac7af" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x(t)={t}^{4}+\\frac{{t}^{3}}{3}+3{t}^{2}+\\frac{20}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289968} :answer-field/id #uuid "beacb1b4-e6ff-5b29-848e-cb0c8fe781e1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289965}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "47fb04f1-6892-5e82-9539-204f7e5c883c" :question/math-academy-id "q-6255" :question/problem "A particle moves along a straight line relative to a fixed origin $O$ with velocity $v(t)=4{t}^{3}+{t}^{2}+6t,$ where $t$ is the time. If the particle is at the position $x=11$ when $t=1,$ calculate the position $x$ of the particle at time $t>0.$" :question/worked-solution "We start by integrating the velocity to get $x(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(t) & =\\int v(t)\\,dt \\\\  & =\\int (4{t}^{3}+{t}^{2}+6t)dt \\\\  & ={t}^{4}+\\frac{{t}^{3}}{3}+3{t}^{2}+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo determine $C,$ we use the fact that $x(1)=11.$ Substituting this into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}11 & =(1)^{4}+\\frac{(1)^{3}}{3}+3(1)^{2}+C \\\\ 11 & =1+\\frac{1}{3}+3+C \\\\ C & =\\frac{20}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the position $x$ of the particle at time $t$ is \n\n$$\nx(t)={t}^{4}+\\frac{{t}^{3}}{3}+3{t}^{2}+\\frac{20}{3}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064852 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "0c234eb8-4a5b-52e1-8854-c183b68184bd" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Position of a Particle Given Its Velocity and Initial Position"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b03e6f01-2787-5ba3-b97e-73da9aa7f86d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x(t) = 3t^{2} + 2t^{3} - 1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186063623} {:answer/id #uuid "b733bc2d-eac0-5e54-92dd-93d059dc3760" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x(t) = 3t^{2} + 2t^{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186063624} {:answer/id #uuid "dd360ec9-21f4-5c8b-8de8-af522c7b3c18" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x(t) = 3t^{2} + 2t^{3} - 2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186063625} {:answer/id #uuid "e95e542e-8f9b-5637-a4db-90f7c8bea670" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x(t) = 3t^{2} + 2t^{3} + 1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186063626} {:answer/id #uuid "192102b0-c951-54cd-94e7-2a8dce6f617c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x(t) = 3t^{2} + 2t^{3} + 2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186063627}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "192102b0-c951-54cd-94e7-2a8dce6f617c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x(t) = 3t^{2} + 2t^{3} + 2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186063627} :answer-field/id #uuid "373c48b0-ac00-598b-9c44-85a45cb3d080" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186063622}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "1d57deba-f2e4-5693-a811-867d6077d98a" :question/math-academy-id "q-7110" :question/problem "A particle moves along a straight line relative to a fixed origin $O$ with velocity $v(t)=6t+6{t}^{2},$ where $t>0$ is the time. If the particle is at the position $x=2$ when $t=0,$ calculate the position $x$ of the particle at time $t.$" :question/requires-calculator true :question/worked-solution "We start by integrating the velocity to get $x(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(t) & =\\int v(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\int (6t+6{t}^{2})\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =3{t}^{2}+2{t}^{3}+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo determine $C$, we use the fact that $x(0)=2.$ Substituting this into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2 & =3(0)^{2}+2(0)^{3}+C \\\\ C & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the position $x$ of the particle at time $t$ is \n\n$$\nx(t)=3{t}^{2}+2{t}^{3}+2.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186289971 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "43e68991-37f0-543e-a4da-b78b395a8494" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Position of a Particle Given Its Acceleration, Initial Velocity and Initial Position"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "283c4063-e17d-59d7-b05f-3aeab7b48772" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x(t)=\\frac{1}{2}{t}^{2}-{t}^{3}+4t+2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289973} {:answer/id #uuid "b109b242-4d91-5866-b06c-c0c243684404" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x(t)=\\frac{1}{2}{t}^{2}+{t}^{3}+6t-2." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289974} {:answer/id #uuid "b5d1caed-313e-5978-a848-16b8e6ea03d0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x(t)={t}^{2}+{t}^{3}+4t+2." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289975} {:answer/id #uuid "25055ab8-5116-58f3-870f-cb322f8954e6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x(t)={t}^{2}-{t}^{3}+2t+2." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289976} {:answer/id #uuid "ae20499b-c11b-5052-83f0-daa09f68f7e1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x(t)=\\frac{1}{2}{t}^{2}-{t}^{3}+6t." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289977}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "283c4063-e17d-59d7-b05f-3aeab7b48772" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x(t)=\\frac{1}{2}{t}^{2}-{t}^{3}+4t+2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289973} :answer-field/id #uuid "ff3680c8-8c21-5564-bf81-58067a900364" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289972}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "13736185-7d14-5f0f-bf06-55c5bf19c2a5" :question/math-academy-id "q-7111" :question/problem "A particle moves along a straight line relative to a fixed origin $O$ with acceleration $a(t)=1-6t,$ where $t\\ge 0$ is the time. If the particle has position $x=2$ and velocity $v=4$ when $t=0,$ calculate the position $x$ of the particle at time $t>0.$" :question/worked-solution "We start by integrating the acceleration to get the velocity $v(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =\\int a(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\int (1-6t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =t-3{t}^{2}+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo determine $C,$ we use the fact that $v(0)=4.$ Substituting this into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4 & =(0)-3(0)^{2}+C \\\\ C & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo the velocity is given by $v(t)=t-3{t}^{2}+4.$\n\nNow, we integrate the velocity to get $x(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(t) & =\\int v(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\int (t-3{t}^{2}+4)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{t}^{2}-{t}^{3}+4t+K\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo determine $K,$ we use the fact that $x(0)=2.$ Substituting this into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2 & =\\frac{1}{2}(0)^{2}-(0)^{3}+4(0)+K \\\\ K & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the position $x$ of the particle at time $t$ is \n\n$$\nx(t)=\\frac{1}{2}{t}^{2}-{t}^{3}+4t+2.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186289978 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "0c234eb8-4a5b-52e1-8854-c183b68184bd" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Position of a Particle Given Its Velocity and Initial Position"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "aa5ac2ba-8edd-51a5-b31a-0a77e30bebb7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x(t)={t}^{3}+{t}^{2}+3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289980} {:answer/id #uuid "153b7060-1af7-5df8-8386-280aa2d65270" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x(t)={t}^{3}+{t}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289981} {:answer/id #uuid "b1cd59fe-630b-55eb-8393-a803f40c26a7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x(t)=4t+7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289982} {:answer/id #uuid "cc07f719-7fb0-5ccc-a345-3d7a9e10665d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x(t)=6t+3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289983} {:answer/id #uuid "d334e6f8-69dd-59fc-9e62-60d42cdd6b5a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x(t)={t}^{3}+{t}^{2}-5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289984}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "aa5ac2ba-8edd-51a5-b31a-0a77e30bebb7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x(t)={t}^{3}+{t}^{2}+3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289980} :answer-field/id #uuid "0ddf3d81-e826-57d4-8134-c53e47130c3f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289979}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "fdf1d19a-20e1-526f-8082-40001a3f405c" :question/math-academy-id "q-7283" :question/problem "A particle moves along a straight line relative to a fixed origin $O$ with velocity $v(t)=3{t}^{2}+2t,$ where $t$ is the time. If the particle is at the position $x=15$ when $t=2,$ calculate the position $x$ of the particle at time $t>0.$" :question/worked-solution "We start by integrating the velocity to get $x(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(t) & =\\int v(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\int (3{t}^{2}+2t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={t}^{3}+{t}^{2}+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo determine $C,$ we use the fact that $x(2)=15.$ Substituting this into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}15 & =(2)^{3}+(2)^{2}+C \\\\ 15 & =8+4+C \\\\ C & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the position $x$ of the particle at time $t$ is \n\n$$\nx(t)={t}^{3}+{t}^{2}+3.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186289985 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "0c234eb8-4a5b-52e1-8854-c183b68184bd" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Position of a Particle Given Its Velocity and Initial Position"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f4ceefa5-decf-57a6-b7a0-0c434c1bee25" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x(t)=\\frac{1}{8}{t}^{2}-\\frac{1}{3}{t}^{3}-\\frac{63}{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289987} {:answer/id #uuid "a8631ccd-4695-50b3-8a92-be83b8e2d24f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x(t)=\\frac{1}{8}{t}^{2}-\\frac{1}{3}{t}^{3}+1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289988} {:answer/id #uuid "883ca621-8638-5983-a96d-4fe709aa8112" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x(t)=\\frac{1}{8}{t}^{2}-\\frac{1}{3}{t}^{3}+\\frac{7}{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289989} {:answer/id #uuid "5f6e578b-a181-58fb-bb19-8e8b2aaad005" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x(t)=\\frac{1}{8}{t}^{2}-\\frac{1}{3}{t}^{3}+4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289990} {:answer/id #uuid "d9bb6baa-db02-5570-86e1-600085365d37" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x(t)=\\frac{1}{4}-2t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289991}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "883ca621-8638-5983-a96d-4fe709aa8112" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x(t)=\\frac{1}{8}{t}^{2}-\\frac{1}{3}{t}^{3}+\\frac{7}{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289989} :answer-field/id #uuid "8acd796b-65df-53ad-8fea-bf76684284d3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289986}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "7529948b-5a79-5722-a1f5-bf453f077a49" :question/math-academy-id "q-7284" :question/problem "A particle moves along a straight line relative to a fixed origin $O$ with velocity $v(t)=\\frac{1}{4}t-{t}^{2},$ where $t>0$ is the time. If the particle is at the position $x=-7$ when $t=3,$ calculate the position $x$ of the particle at time $t.$" :question/worked-solution "We start by integrating the velocity to get $x(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(t) & =\\int v(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\int (\\frac{1}{4}t-{t}^{2})\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{8}{t}^{2}-\\frac{1}{3}{t}^{3}+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo determine $C,$ we use the fact that $x(3)=-7.$ Substituting this into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-7 & =\\frac{1}{8}(3)^{2}-\\frac{1}{3}(3)^{3}+C \\\\ -7 & =-\\frac{63}{8}+C \\\\ C & =\\frac{7}{8}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the position $x$ of the particle at time $t$ is \n\n$$\nx(t)=\\frac{1}{8}{t}^{2}-\\frac{1}{3}{t}^{3}+\\frac{7}{8}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186289992 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "43e68991-37f0-543e-a4da-b78b395a8494" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Position of a Particle Given Its Acceleration, Initial Velocity and Initial Position"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d6547d14-3742-5628-9104-81391c0682e9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x(t)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)+4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289994} {:answer/id #uuid "53fb6580-320e-5947-9b68-c2238cf9f2d2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x(t)=t+\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)+5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289995} {:answer/id #uuid "f2480a20-1cea-56e1-a6c2-24ac3c7b1ec5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x(t)=2t-3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)+8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289996} {:answer/id #uuid "97336569-bc9a-5185-837d-0317e9002304" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x(t)=2t-\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)+5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289997} {:answer/id #uuid "fb9c4b90-9108-58d3-88c0-ef8beab10393" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x(t)=2t+3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)+5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289998}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "97336569-bc9a-5185-837d-0317e9002304" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x(t)=2t-\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)+5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289997} :answer-field/id #uuid "194a0fc3-e330-575c-85b7-ba6b49b567d2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289993}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "d0b98d95-03af-598a-9e95-71e4d8b5a534" :question/math-academy-id "q-81441" :question/problem "A particle moves along a straight line relative to a fixed origin $O$ with acceleration $a(t)=3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t),$ where $t\\ge 0$ is the time. If the particle has position $x=5$ and velocity $v=1$ when $t=0,$ calculate the position $x$ of the particle at time $t.$" :question/worked-solution "We start by integrating the acceleration to get the velocity $v(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =\\int a(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\int 3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)\\text{d}t \\\\  & =-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo determine $C,$ we use the fact that $v(0)=1.$ Substituting this into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}1 & =-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3(0))+C \\\\ 1 & =-1+C \\\\ C & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo the velocity is given by $v(t)=2-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t).$\n\nNow, we integrate the velocity to get $x(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(t) & =\\int v(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\int (2-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t))\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2t-\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)+K\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo determine $K,$ we use the fact that $x(0)=5.$ Substituting this into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5 & =2(0)-\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3(0))+K \\\\ K & =5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the position $x$ of the particle at time $t$ is \n\n$$\nx(t)=2t-\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)+5.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186289999 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "43e68991-37f0-543e-a4da-b78b395a8494" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Position of a Particle Given Its Acceleration, Initial Velocity and Initial Position"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "fd91ab91-5062-5dbc-8ee3-cbcbdc22bbbb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x(t)=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)-2t+3\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186290001} {:answer/id #uuid "0f998b3e-512e-5a38-9369-447e501d61bd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x(t)=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t)+2t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186290002} {:answer/id #uuid "09154eac-a091-51cd-9bcd-01505287cd20" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x(t)=-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)+2t+\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186290003} {:answer/id #uuid "3bc75397-04fe-5142-95a6-3d79a59998ea" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x(t)=-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)+4t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186290004} {:answer/id #uuid "a6831d72-7e48-59cf-9c98-9602655059df" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x(t)=-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t)+2t+\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186290005}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "09154eac-a091-51cd-9bcd-01505287cd20" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x(t)=-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)+2t+\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186290003} :answer-field/id #uuid "e8f49fdf-2810-54d5-9246-8a3b730d3fdb" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186290000}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "641e7b63-702c-540a-b9fd-6b417e1a8c11" :question/math-academy-id "q-84244" :question/problem "A particle moves along a straight line relative to a fixed origin $O$ with acceleration $a(t)=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t),$ where $t\\ge 0$ is the time. If the particle has position $x=2\\pi $ and velocity $v=4$ when $t=\\frac{\\pi }{2},$ calculate the position $x$ of the particle at time $t.$" :question/worked-solution "We start by integrating the acceleration to get the velocity $v(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =\\int a(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\int 2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t)+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo determine $C,$ we use the fact that $v(\\frac{\\pi }{2})=4.$ Substituting this into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4 & =2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})+C \\\\ 4 & =2(1)+C \\\\ C & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo the velocity is given by $v(t)=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t)+2.$\n\nNow, we integrate the velocity to get $x(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(t) & =\\int v(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\int (2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t)+2)\\text{d}t \\\\  & =-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)+2t+K\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo determine $K,$ we use the fact that $x(\\frac{\\pi }{2})=2\\pi .$ Substituting this into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2\\pi  & =-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})+2(\\frac{\\pi }{2})+K \\\\ 2\\pi  & =-2(0)+\\pi +K \\\\ K & =\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the position $x$ of the particle at time $t$ is \n\n$$\nx(t)=-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)+2t+\\pi .\n$$"}]}
